# Native gfx11 form of gemm_fp8a8_kernel, the block-fp8 GEMM (gemm_fp8a8_nt_m16, its gated twin and
# gemm_fp8a8_tiled): the trunk of an 8-bit Qwen3.8-27B.
#
# Why: through the compatibility layer two RX 7900 XTX serve the block-fp8 27B at 7.7 tok/s decode and about 200
# tok/s prefill (run fp8a-tp2, 2026-10-10) against 22.8 and about 2,750 on one R9700 with libr4d. Every fp8 x fp8
# WMMA widens 16 codes through f32, moves 4 dwords of operands and 4 of accumulator across lanes.
#
# libr4d: both operands are E4M3 bytes; B is always staged in shared memory (the activation on the narrow path, the
# weight on the prefill tile), A is staged (prefill, the activation) or held in registers in fragment order (narrow,
# the weight). A lane reads 8 bytes of each and calls the gfx12 fp8 WMMA.
#
# Native (the fp8 instantiations only; the int8 ones keep the compatibility layer):
#   - codes are widened to IEEE half and multiplied by the f16 WMMA: a half built from a code's own bits is the
#     code's value times 2^-8 for every code, subnormals included, so widening is a few bit operations
#     (r3_compat.h, "block fp8"). The first native form widened to bf16 and held the narrow path at 139 GB/s of
#     weight (run fp8b-prof: gate/up 321 us a call) where the MXFP4 kernel reaches about 430.
#   - B is staged as halves, widened once as it is written. On the prefill tile B is the weight and eight waves read
#     every staged row, so this is the conversion that is shared; a B fragment is then one 32-byte load.
#   - A stays as libr4d has it and is widened where it is read, one fragment a wave a K step: 16 bytes of its row
#     from shared memory, or the lane's 8 codes with its partner's 8. Staging A as halves too would not fit: the
#     prefill tile would need 82 KiB of shared memory.
#   - the gfx11 WMMA takes the accumulator as its C operand and the accumulator stays in the gfx11 layout through
#     the K loop. The fold at a 128-column boundary is per slot, so it only has to name the gfx11 row of a slot
#     where the scale is per A row; `out` is moved to the gfx12 layout once, in front of the unchanged epilogue,
#     and multiplied by 2^16 there.
#   - ONE TOKEN ROW IS NOT A MATRIX PRODUCT. On the narrow path with one row of activation, a 16x16 WMMA tile does
#     one column of useful work in sixteen, and the kernel reads 337 to 523 GB/s of weight on an XTX where the
#     single-row vector kernels (gemm_fp8a16_nt_m1, logits_gemm_fp8) read 660 to 790 (run bw-xtx, 2026-10-10). So
#     with nb == 1 the K loop is replaced by a dot product: a lane multiplies the 32 weight bytes it owns in each
#     fragment-order tile by the activation bytes at the same K, the pair of lanes that share a weight row add
#     their halves, and the sixteen row sums are placed in the two lanes that hold the token's column in the gfx12
#     accumulator, so the epilogue (split-K, the gate, the store) is unchanged. The weight stream is the same
#     coalesced 1 KiB a wave a step.

@@ find 1
